.TH INDI_SCOPELINK 1 "2026-08-12" "indi-scopelink 1.0" "INDI Drivers"
.SH NAME
indi_scopelink \- INDI driver for the Astrolabs ScopeLink telescope controller
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B indiserver
.RI [ options ]
.B indi_scopelink
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B indi_scopelink
publishes one Astrolabs ScopeLink controller as a single INDI device. It is a driver, not a program to
run by hand: an INDI server starts it and clients such as KStars or Ekos talk to it over the server.
.PP
One USB connection carries a stepper focuser, a field rotator, a motorised front flap over the telescope
aperture, a flat panel, two mirror cooling fans and, depending on the hardware generation, two auxiliary
power outputs and a powered USB hub. The driver claims the INDI light box and auxiliary interfaces on
every controller; the focuser, rotator and dust cap interfaces are claimed for whichever of them the
connected controller turns out to have a motor assigned to and calibrated for.
.PP
What a controller offers is read out of the hardware when the driver connects, so properties that a given
generation does not have are never published rather than being published and failing. From interface 1.1
that includes which motor drives what, which the controller holds as a setting rather than having wired:
the driver reads the assignment on connect and offers the devices it names.
.SH USAGE
Start the driver under an INDI server:
.PP
.RS
.B indiserver -v indi_scopelink
.RE
.PP
Then connect a client and set
.B DEVICE_PORT
to the controller's port. Anything but
.IR /dev/ttyACM n,
which is renumbered whenever another CDC device is plugged in first: the udev rule names a controller
.I /dev/scopelink
from its USB identifiers, and where more than one is plugged in, the per-unit name under
.I /dev/serial/by-id/
tells them apart. A controller flashed before those identifiers were assigned has no
.I /dev/scopelink
and is reached by its by-id name.
.SH MOTORS
Up to interface 1.0 the motors were wired to their jobs: motor 1 focused, and motor 2 where there was one
opened the front flap. From 1.1 the controller holds the mapping as a setting instead \- which motor
focuses, which rotates, and which ones the flap is made of, in the order they open. The driver reads that
assignment when it connects, names it in the log, and offers a focuser, a rotator or a dust cap only
where the assignment names a motor and that motor has a calibrated travel.
.PP
The assignment is edited on the Parameters tab, under
.BR Focuser ,
.B Rotator
and
.BR "Front flap" ,
and the settings offered are the motors the connected controller actually has. Its rules are the
controller's own: nothing may name a motor that is not fitted, the flap parts must be a run without a gap
in it, and no motor may be claimed by two functions.
.PP
A change needs the controller restarted, not just reconnected. The controller reads its configuration when
it starts and drives from that copy, so an assignment written while it runs is stored but not acted on \-
it goes on driving the one it started with, and refuses a command for anything the new one adds.
.PP
.B Restart controller
on the Parameters tab does it: the controller restarts, the link drops for the few seconds it takes to
leave the USB bus and come back, and the driver connects again by itself. That last part matters, because
the devices published are settled at connection \- a client that has been handed a focuser cannot have a
different one put underneath it.
.PP
The Calibration tab addresses a motor rather than a job, on every controller, and lists every motor the
controller has labelled with what it currently drives.
.SH FIRMWARE
The Firmware tab replaces the controller's firmware. Put the path of the encrypted file supplied for this
particular unit in
.BR FIRMWARE_FILE ,
then use
.B Check the file
to have it examined and
.B Install firmware
to write it. Every firmware file is named after the unit it belongs to, and one that is not is refused
before the controller is touched.
.PP
No other software is needed. The controller carries a boot loader of its own which appears as the same
serial port the firmware does, so the whole update is an ordinary conversation on the port the driver is
already connected to \- there is no DFU mode, no second device on the bus, and nothing to run as root.
The driver does not decrypt the file: the boot loader checks the container as it arrives and verifies the
result against a checksum read back out of the flash the firmware landed in.
.PP
The update refuses to start while a motor is moving, and the device cannot be disconnected while one is
running. Only the firmware area is erased \- the configuration is kept elsewhere and is expected to
survive \- but the driver saves the whole configuration to
.I ~/.indi/
first, and puts back afterwards anything the new firmware does not already have right.
.PP
An update that is interrupted leaves the controller in its boot loader with no firmware to run. It still
answers, and connecting to it says so and offers the Firmware tab and nothing else, which is what
recovers it: install the firmware again.
.PP
Firmware updates are not simulated. The one question worth asking of an update is whether a real
controller survives it, and that is the part a simulated port cannot answer.
.SH SIMULATION
Enabling the standard INDI
.B SIMULATION
switch before connecting makes the driver answer itself, with no port opened.
.B SIMULATED_GENERATION
on the Options tab selects which controller it imitates, which is what makes the differences between them
visible. There are four entries for three generations: the same generation 3 board runs interface 1.0 and
1.1, and 1.1 is a different frame layout rather than a different board. See
.BR scopelink-simulator (1)
for the same simulated controller served on a pseudo terminal instead.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/indi/indi_scopelink.xml
Driver list entry, which is how clients find the driver.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-scopelink.rules
Access rights for the controller, and the
.I /dev/scopelink
name.
.TP
.I ~/.indi/ScopeLink_config.xml
Saved device configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.indi/scopelink-<unit>-<timestamp>.par
Configuration saved before a firmware update, in the format the vendor's Windows parameter editor reads.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scopelink-cli (1),
.BR scopelink-simulator (1),
.BR indiserver (1)
.PP
Full documentation, including the Ekos walkthrough, is at
.UR https://drivers.indilib.org/focusers/astrolabs/scopelink/scopelink
.UE .
